            <div xml:id="d693">

                <pb facs="0831" n="757" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v01/pg757"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111N39/72</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-11-29T11:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 29, 1917, 11 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 1.55 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7832. Your 5895, November 24, 3 p.m. Lord Robert Cecil, in charge Foreign Office
                    during Balfour’s absence in Paris, informs me that his Government has no
                    objection to such a declaration by Government of the United States, but he
                    thinks it better that all principal Allied powers should make such a declaration
                    at same time if any make it. A declaration by one great power might suggest
                    suspicion that others were not willing to make declaration. He declared the
                    British Government quite willing to make declaration and he expressed hope that
                    the French would also.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
